{
  "id": "lowfield-itbs-pattern",
  "name": "iTBS pattern (Huang 2005 timing) at F3",
  "category": "lowfield",
  "evidence": "unverified",
  "fidelity": "timing",
  "dose": {"value": [500, 2000], "unit": "mT", "plane": "unstated", "cite": "Dufor 2019 p.1"},
  "origin": "Huang & Rothwell (UCL) 2005; clinical iTBS (THREE-D, Toronto/Vancouver; SNT, Stanford)",
  "uT": 5,
  "summary": "Copies intermittent theta-burst timing (Huang et al. 2005): pulse triplets at 50 Hz repeated at 5 Hz in 2-s trains every 10 s, 20 trains and 600 pulses, on one coil at F3, a scalp approximation of the left DLPFC. TMS works at 0.5 to 2 tesla; this helmet is at least 60,000 times weaker at 20 mm. This is a rhythm pattern, not theta-burst stimulation.",
  "notes": "Suppa et al. 2008 define iTBS as 3 pulses at 50 Hz repeated at 5 Hz, 20 trains of 2 s every 10 s (600 pulses, usually quoted as 190 s; strictly 192 s). Huang 2005 used 80% of active motor threshold. The THREE-D depression trial (414 patients; iTBS non-inferior to 10 Hz rTMS) gave iTBS at 120% of resting motor threshold, aimed by neuronavigation at MNI152 (x -38, y +44, z +26), not at F3 (Blumberger 2018 p.3 [1685]). This program plays the same timing on one coil at F3 with no fade, so all 20 trains run at full strength. F3 is a scalp approximation of the left DLPFC. Fox et al. 2012 found skull-landmark sites (F3, the 5-cm rule) the least anticorrelated with the subgenual cingulate. Each TMS pulse (about 300-us biphasic) becomes +A for 0.5 ms then -A for 0.5 ms, since shorter shapes cannot be resolved at 2 kHz. Dufor 2019 states the general range of TMS as '0.5 to 2 Tesla' (p.1), with no place of measurement. For the electric field TMS induces, the sources give 90 V/m (Larsen 2020 p.3 [2]) and more than 500 V/m under the coil (Rohan 2004 p.3 [95]). This program's setting is the peak field 20 mm in front of each coil. There the helmet is 60,000 to 240,000 times weaker than a TMS coil, even at its maximum. The evidence for iTBS belongs to TMS strength. Low-intensity iTBS was tested at mT strength only: Tang 2016 used 190 s at 85 mT in vitro (p.4), and Dufor 2019 ran 10 min with 1800 pulses per session in rodent work (p.2), not one 192-s block. No data exist at uT. It is not a treatment. Do not copy accelerated clinical schedules; there is no evidence for them at uT. Not for people with epilepsy; stop if you feel unwell.",
  "setting": "Seated, awake.",
  "schedule": "One 192-s block (600 pulses) per sitting. Do not copy accelerated clinical schedules; nothing is known at uT.",
  "targets": ["left-dlpfc"],
  "source": "Huang et al. 2005, Neuron https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15664172/ ; Suppa et al. 2008, J Physiol (iTBS and cTBS definitions) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2614023/ ; Blumberger et al. 2018, Lancet (THREE-D) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29726344/ ; Fox et al. 2012 (left DLPFC targets) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4120275/ ; Cole et al. 2022, Am J Psychiatry (SNT) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34711062/ ; Tang et al. 2016, Neuroscience (low-intensity iTBS in vitro) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27568058/ ; Dufor et al. 2019, Sci Adv (rodent low-intensity rTMS) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6821463/",
